IN PLAIN WORDS
A view function can return plain text, an HTML snippet (the browser renders the tags), or a whole file with send_file("index.html"). Flask labels returned strings as Content-Type: text/html.

CODE
```python
from flask import Flask, send_file

app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route("/")
def home():
    return send_file("index.html")                  # a whole HTML file

@app.route("/about")
def about():
    return "<h1>About this Movies API</h1><p>Served from a Flask app</p>"   # an HTML snippet

@app.route("/plain")
def plain():
    return "Just some text"                         # no tags → shows as plain text
```

KEY POINTS
- Content-Type tells the browser what kind of data it received.
- A string with tags renders as HTML; without tags it simply shows as text.

IN PLAIN WORDS
Validate before changing anything. Guard clauses return 400 for a body that isn't an object, missing fields, empty strings, a year that isn't a number, or watched that isn't true/false. Use the same error shape every time so the front end can show data.message.

STEPS
1. data = request.get_json(silent=True)
2. Not a dict → 400.
3. Any required field missing → 400.
4. title and director must be non-empty strings → else 400.
5. year must convert to an int → else 400.
6. watched must be a bool → else 400.
7. Only then create, append and return 201.

CODE
```python
def bad_request(message):
    return jsonify({"error": "Bad Request", "message": message}), 400

data = request.get_json(silent=True)
if not isinstance(data, dict):
    return bad_request("Request body must be a JSON object.")
for field in ("title", "director", "year", "watched"):
    if field not in data:
        return bad_request(f"Missing required field: {field}")
for field in ("title", "director"):
    if not isinstance(data[field], str) or not data[field].strip():
        return bad_request(f"Field '{field}' must be a non-empty string.")
if isinstance(data["year"], bool):
    return bad_request("Field 'year' must be a number.")
try:
    year_value = int(data["year"])
except (TypeError, ValueError):
    return bad_request("Field 'year' must be a number.")
if not isinstance(data["watched"], bool):
    return bad_request("Field 'watched' must be a boolean.")
```

```javascript
// front end: <form id="form" novalidate> and show the API's message
if (status === 400) {
  $("#formErr").textContent = data?.message || "Invalid input.";
  return;
}
```

KEY POINTS
- Guard clauses: return early with 400 the moment something is wrong.
- 400 = bad input. 404 = missing resource.
- novalidate turns off the browser's own checks, so the API's message is what people see.

CORRECTION
- With plain get_json(), bodies that aren't JSON were stopped by Flask (415 or 400) before your check, so the front end never got your message. silent=True lets your check handle every case.
- int(True) is 1 in Python, so "year": true would have been saved as year 1. The bool check above blocks it.

IN PLAIN WORDS
Capstone: a spells API. /health plus full CRUD on /spells, the same error shape for 400 and 404 (message can be a list), one validate_spell() helper shared by POST and PUT, a global next_id counter, and Swagger docs at /apidocs grouped with tags.

KEY IDEAS
1. Spell fields: id (auto), name, element, power (non-empty strings), mana_cost and tier (numbers), is_ultimate (boolean).
2. One validation helper returns (ok, errors); POST and PUT both use it.
3. Errors: {"error": "Bad Request" or "Not Found", "message": text or a list of texts}.
4. @app.get / @app.post / @app.put / @app.delete are shortcuts for @app.route(..., methods=[...]).

CODE
```python
REQUIRED_FIELDS = ["name", "element", "mana_cost", "power", "tier", "is_ultimate"]

def is_number(value):
    return isinstance(value, (int, float)) and not isinstance(value, bool)

def validate_spell(payload):
    if not isinstance(payload, dict):
        return False, ["Payload must be a JSON object."]
    errors = [f"Missing required field: {f}" for f in REQUIRED_FIELDS if f not in payload]
    if errors:
        return False, errors
    for field in ("name", "element", "power"):
        if not isinstance(payload[field], str) or not payload[field].strip():
            errors.append(f"Field '{field}' must be a non-empty string.")
    for field in ("mana_cost", "tier"):
        if not is_number(payload[field]):
            errors.append(f"Field '{field}' must be a number.")
    if not isinstance(payload["is_ultimate"], bool):
        errors.append("Field 'is_ultimate' must be a boolean.")
    return not errors, errors

@app.post("/spells")
def create_spell():
    global next_id
    data = request.get_json(silent=True)
    ok, errors = validate_spell(data)
    if not ok:
        return {"error": "Bad Request", "message": errors}, 400
    new_spell = {"id": next_id, **{f: data[f] for f in REQUIRED_FIELDS}}
    spells.append(new_spell)
    next_id += 1
    return new_spell, 201
```

```text
GET    /health          → 200 {"status": "ok", "service": "spells_api"}
GET    /spells          → 200 {"spells": [...]}
GET    /spells/<id>     → 200 spell | 404
POST   /spells          → 201 new spell | 400
PUT    /spells/<id>     → 200 updated | 400 | 404
DELETE /spells/<id>     → 204 | 404
```

KEY POINTS
- 200 with body, 201 created, 204 no body, 400 bad input, 404 missing.
- tags: groups endpoints in the docs.

NOTE
In Python, True and False count as int, so isinstance(True, (int, float)) is True and "mana_cost": true would have passed the lesson's check. is_number() above rules booleans out.
